    "summary": "Stellar Eyes of the World jam and tight set, minor pitch quirks",
    "blurb": "The show delivers strong performances of They Love Each Other, Eyes Of The World and Here Comes Sunshine, with Jerry’s soaring solos and Phil’s thumping bass. The second set builds momentum through extended jams, highlighted by a flowing Weather Report Suite and seamless transitions into China Doll.",
    "review": "Winterland on November 9, 1973 captures the Dead in high‑energy form. Jerry Garcia’s tone is on point, delivering loopy licks during Eyes Of The World and powerful leads throughout the set. Phil Lesh provides a thumping, harmonically rich bass foundation that locks the band together, while Bob Weir supplies solid rhythm work with occasional standout fills—especially on I Know You Rider. Keith Godchaux’s piano and organ shine on Here Comes Sunshine and Me And My Uncle, adding bright texture to the mix. The second set is a showcase of extended improvisation: a soaring Weather Report Suite that segues into Let It Grow, then into an expansive Eyes Of The World before flowing straight into China Doll. Despite some speed/pitch quirks in the soundboard recording that require a -2.5 % correction and occasional mono punches, the overall balance is good and the performance energy shines through.",
